    "alert_id": "ALERT_TDF_TOXICITY_PROXIMAL_TUBULOPATHY",
    "title": "TDF toxicity: low phosphate, proteinuria, bone pain",
    "message": "Guidelines list TDF major toxicities including chronic kidney disease, Fanconi syndrome, decreased bone mineral density, lower back pain, bone aches, and muscle cramps, with management requiring LFTs and RFTs. Patient has taken TDF for 6 years and presents with lower back pain, heavy thigh sensation, serum phosphate 0.78 mmol/L, and trace proteinuria. Evidence supporting TDF attribution: low phosphate with proteinuria is consistent with proximal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi spectrum), and musculoskeletal symptoms match listed TDF toxicities. Evidence against: creatinine is within normal limits, back pain may be occupational from truck driving, and proteinuria could be secondary to uncontrolled hypertension. The combination of hypophosphataemia plus proteinuria in a long-term TDF user warrants evaluation for TDF-induced tubulopathy.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Perform renal function tests including eGFR, urine protein-to-creatinine ratio, and serum phosphate trend. If tubulopathy confirmed, consider substituting TDF with TAF or alternative per guidelines.",
