    "alert_id": "ALERT_IBUPROFEN_TDF_NSAID_NEPHROTOXICITY",
    "title": "Ibuprofen plus TDF: compounding nephrotoxicity risk",
    "message": "Guidelines list TDF as a cause of acute kidney injury and chronic kidney disease. The patient uses over-the-counter ibuprofen as needed for chronic back pain while on long-term TDF. NSAIDs are independently nephrotoxic and can precipitate acute kidney injury, particularly in patients with pre-existing renal tubular dysfunction. The patient already shows trace proteinuria and low phosphate, suggesting subclinical renal tubular involvement. Concurrent ibuprofen use compounds this risk. The clinician's plan recommends PRN ibuprofen without flagging this interaction.",
    "issue_type": "drug_interaction",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Counsel patient to minimise ibuprofen use. Consider non-NSAID analgesia such as paracetamol. Monitor renal function closely if ibuprofen cannot be avoided.",
